00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:25.350
So let's go through part of the forward propagation calculation for a few examples.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:37.630
Let's say that for the first training example, you end up computing this X1 plus B1, and then

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:49.740
for the second training example, you end up computing this X2 plus B1, and then for the

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:57.380
third training example, you end up computing this X3 plus B1.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.500
So just to simplify the explanation on this slide, I'm going to ignore B. So let's just

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:08.860
say, you know, for the- to simplify this justification a little bit, that B is equal to 0.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.900
But the argument I'm going to lay out will work with just a little bit of a change, even

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:13.900
when B is non-zero.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.700
This is just to simplify the description on this slide a bit.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.300
Now W1 is going to be some matrix, right?

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.920
So I have some number of rows in this matrix.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:38.120
So if you look at this calculation X1, what you have is that W1 times X1 gives you some

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.680
column vector, which I'm going to draw like this.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:56.890
And similarly, if you look at this vector X2, you have that W1 times X2 gives some

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.780
other column vector, right?

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.980
And that's, you know, gives you this, I guess, Z1, 2.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:17.020
And finally, if you look at X3, you have W1 times X3 gives you some third column vector.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.740
That's this Z1, 3.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:29.500
So now, if you consider the trading set capital X, which we formed by stacking together all

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.800
of our training examples.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:38.780
So the matrix capital X is formed by taking the vector X1 and stacking it vertically with

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:44.100
X2, and then also X3.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.060
This is if we have only three training examples, if we have more, you know, there'll be a-

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.380
we'll keep stacking horizontally like that.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:58.340
But if you now take this matrix X and multiply it by W, then you end up with, if you think

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:03.260
about how matrix multiplication works, you end up with the first column being these

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:06.540
same values that had drawn up there in purple.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.220
The second column will be those same four values.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:19.740
And the third column will be those orange values, what they turn out to be.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:33.800
But of course, this is just equal to Z1, 1 expressed as a column vector, followed by Z1, 2 expressed

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:39.480
as a column vector, followed by Z1, 3 also expressed as a column vector.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.640
And this is if you have three training examples, if you have more examples, then there'll be

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:44.440
more columns.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:51.680
And so this is just our matrix capital Z1.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:04:05.920
So I hope this gives a justification for why when we had previously W1 times Xi equals Z1i.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.880
When we're looking at a single training example at a time, when you took the different training

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.600
examples and stacked them up in different columns, then the corresponding result is that

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:19.280
you end up with the Z's also stacked up in columns.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.840
And I won't show, but you can convince yourself if you want that with Python broadcasting.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:30.880
If you add back in these values of B, the values are still correct.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:36.160
What actually ends up happening is you end up with Python broadcasting, you end up adding

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.040
Bi individually to each of the columns of this matrix.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:53.840
So on this slide, I've only justified that Z1 equals W1x plus B1 is a correct vectorization

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.280
of the first step of the four steps that we had on the previous slide.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.080
But it turns out that a similar analysis allows you to show that the other steps also work

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:07.560
out using a very similar logic where if you stack the inputs in columns, then after the

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.160
equation you get the corresponding outputs also stacked up in columns.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.240
Finally, let's just recap everything we talked about in this video.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.240
If this is your neural network, we said that this is what you need to do if you were to

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:26.360
implement for a propagation one training example at a time going from i equals 1 through m.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:31.520
And then we said, let's stack up the training examples in columns like so.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:37.600
And for each of these values, Z1, A1, Z2, A2, let's stack up the corresponding columns

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:38.600
as follows.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:48.340
So this example for A1, but this is true for Z1, A1, Z2, and A2.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:55.540
Then what we showed on the previous slide was that this line allows you to vectorize

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:59.060
this across all m examples at the same time.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:03.900
And it turns out with a similar reasoning, you can show that all of the other lines are

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:09.380
correct vectorizations of all four of these lines of code.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:19.460
And just as a reminder, because X is also equal to A0, because remember that the input

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:23.220
feature vector X was equal to A0.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:28.540
So Xi equals A0i, right?

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:33.580
Then there's actually a certain symmetry to these equations where this first equation can

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:41.900
also be written Z1 equals W1A0 plus B1.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:46.500
And so you see that, you know, this pair of equations and this pair of equations actually

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.900
look very similar, but just of all of the indices advance by 1.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.580
So this kind of shows that the different layers of a neural network are, you know, roughly

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.780
doing the same thing or just doing the same computation over and over.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:06.020
And here we have a two-layer neural network where we go to a much deeper neural network

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.580
in next week's videos.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:13.020
You see that even deeper neural networks are basically taking these two steps and just

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.420
doing them even more times than you're seeing here.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:21.580
So that's how you can vectorize your neural network across multiple training examples.

